  "articleBody": "If you notice cracked grout along your shower corners or loose tiles on the floor, ignoring it can lead to costly framing rot beneath the surface. Tile and grout look solid, but they are not a waterproof barrier by themselves. In a wet environment, the real water barrier lives in the waterproofing membrane beneath the mortar. When grout cracks at changes of plane—like where walls meet or where a wall hits the shower floor—it is often because rigid grout was used instead of flexible sealant, or the substrate is shifting.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps:\n- Grout is crumbling across multiple joints or tiles sound hollow when tapped.\n- You spot mildew that keeps returning deep inside the grout lines despite frequent cleaning.\n- You are planning to install heavy natural stone or large-format tiles that demand precise subfloor deflection ratings.\n- The shower pan itself is leaking into the room or ceiling below.\n\nA qualified tile professional inspects the backing material, fixes any underlying framing flex, and ensures a proper membrane is laid according to industry ANSI methods. They also use silica-dust controls during demolition to protect your home's indoor air quality.\n\nIf you are dealing with broken shower tile or water concerns in the Denver metro area, Abraham Becerra and the team at HH Remodeling LLC can inspect your space and rebuild it right. Call 303-505-0866 to discuss your bathroom remodel.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
